---
import SourceList from './SourceList.astro';
import { elfEntryPath } from '../data/manual-structure';

const targets = [
  { name: '68k', machine: 'EM_68K', value: 4, byteOrder: 'Big endian', default: 'Optional' },
  { name: 'ARM', machine: 'EM_ARM', value: 40, byteOrder: 'Little endian', default: 'Enabled by default' },
  { name: 'x32', machine: 'EM_X86_64', value: 62, byteOrder: 'Little endian', default: 'Enabled by default' },
];
---
<section aria-labelledby="elf-executables">
  <h2 id="elf-executables" class="prm-section-heading ruled">ELF executables in pTOS</h2>
  <p><a href="/reference/pexec/">Pexec</a> selects the loader from the file signature. Bytes <code>7F 45 4C 46</code>
    select ELF when <code>CONF_WITH_ELF_LOADER</code> is enabled. The 68k PRG loader uses the signature
    <code>60 1A</code> when <code>CONF_WITH_PRG_LOADER</code> is enabled.</p>
  <table class="prm-rule-table">
    <caption>ELF32 class and machine by target</caption>
    <thead><tr>
      <th scope="col">Target</th><th scope="col"><code>e_machine</code></th>
      <th scope="col">Byte order</th><th scope="col">Static loader default</th>
    </tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {targets.map(target => <tr>
        <th scope="row">{target.name}</th><td><code>{target.machine}</code> ({target.value})</td>
        <td>{target.byteOrder}</td><td>{target.default}</td>
      </tr>)}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>All three targets use <code>ELFCLASS32</code>. The x32 ABI uses x86-64 instructions and 32-bit user pointers.
    An IA-32 (<code>EM_386</code>) or ELF64 executable fails the native x32 header check.</p>

  <table class="prm-rule-table prm-key-table">
    <caption>ELF header and program-header records used at load time</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Field or record</th><th scope="col">Loader behavior</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>e_type = ET_EXEC</code></th>
        <td>Fixed-address executable. When a private address space can preserve the linked address, the static
          loader needs no relocation entries. Retain relocations with <code>ld --emit-relocs</code> if the image must move.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>e_type = ET_DYN</code></th>
        <td>The static loader accepts a position-independent executable without <code>PT_INTERP</code>.
          GNU <code>ld -pie --no-dynamic-linker</code> produces this form. Retain any relocations the executable needs.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>PT_LOAD</code></th>
        <td>Load <code>p_filesz</code> bytes from file offset <code>p_offset</code> into the segment at <code>p_vaddr</code>
          plus the load bias. <code>p_memsz</code> describes the memory extent, including the zero-filled part.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>e_entry</code></th>
        <td>Application entry address before the load bias is applied. For an interpreted executable, the kernel
          enters the interpreter first and passes the adjusted application entry as <code>AT_ENTRY</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>PT_INTERP</code></th>
        <td>Absolute path to the userspace runtime linker on the boot drive. Requires the experimental
          <code>CONF_WITH_ELF_SHARED</code> option and separate, demand-paged process address spaces.
          Builds without this option refuse files that request an interpreter.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>PT_PTOS_RELOC</code><br /><code>0x60000001</code></th>
        <td>Optional pTOS compact relocation stream produced by <code>ptos-elf-pack</code>. When the static loader
          needs relocations, it uses this stream if present. Otherwise it reads the retained ELF <code>REL</code>
          or <code>RELA</code> sections.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p><code>CONF_WITH_ELF_SHARED</code> defaults off. The interpreter backend supports x32, ARM-state
    hard-float EABI5 binaries on MMU ARM, and 68040 address-space builds. The standard interpreter path
    is <code>/GEMSYS/LIB/<var>arch</var>/LD.SO</code>, where <var>arch</var> is <code>X32</code>, <code>ARM</code>,
    or <code>M68K</code>. A missing interpreter prevents the program from starting.</p>
  <p>See <a href="/subsystems/gemdos/program-launch-and-tpa/#elf-startup">Program launch and TPA</a> for
    placement in memory and <a href={elfEntryPath}>ELF32 process entry</a> for the interpreted startup ABI.</p>
</section>
